WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in 1949 harry s truman was tired world  war ii had just ended and in his second  inaugural address truman needed to  inspire americans weary after years of  war to do it  he painted America and a fresh and  altruistically he said and I quote more  than half the world's population live in  conditions approaching misery their  economic life is primitive and stagnant  we must embark on a bold new program for  the improvement and growth of  underdeveloped areas  what Truman is saying here is a powerful  new idea in the world development if the  West pulls our resources we can help  poor developing countries do just that  develop get richer it's an uplifting  narrative making Americans feel good  that we can help one small issue this  was a total PR play it was a concept  made up ten days prior by a mid-level  State Department employee whose only  mandate was to reignite American energy  for Truman's second term at the time of  the speech not one document existed to  support the actual execution of the plan  and to put this in historical context a  Western led narrative at the time of  benevolence was unprecedented Western  led colonialism was still going on  after World War two at the time of the  speech but Americans loved the new  narrative and we followed through and  today the West mainly US and Europe give  on average a hundred and thirty billion  in aid per year this is through large  philanthropies like the Gates Foundation  through multilaterals like the World  Bank and IMF and through traditional  government aid like USAID or dip it in  the UK and through our collective giving  Truman's narrative has stood the test of  time rich countries help poor countries  it's something we all kind of know but  for this idea to have legs we need to  believe that it's in some way working  that that 130 billion we give in aid per  year is having real impact and the poor  are getting less poor and that too is a  widely touted narrative in 2015 the UN  published a landmark report that said  that the global poverty rate has been  cut in half over the last thirty years  and that sounds great  because that means that our efforts are  working and our global economic system  is sound and we should all feel good  about that if not for a difficult fact  Global Poverty isn't getting better in  fact it hasn't budged in 30 years so the  2015 claim of huge wins against global  poverty relies on an antiquated $2 a day  poverty line that poverty line is set by  the same institutions responsible for  showing progress against poverty and so  that line is abysmal ehlo most  development economists today cite that  it takes closer to an $8 a day poverty  line to achieve basic nutrition and  normal human life expectancy and if we  look at the $8 a day poverty line and  subtract out China which has taken its  own development efforts recently outside  of US control we see that the proportion  of people living in extreme poverty  hasn't meaningfully changed in three  decades and we can see this reflected  again in our ever-growing global  inequality the gap in GDP per capita  between rich and poor countries has  tripled since the time of Truman's  speech let that sink in  this is not a tale of poor countries  catching up to the West this is a story  of a growing poverty divided for me this  is a profound thing to grapple with I  work in development economics and I've  worked to expand access to credit for  the unbanked in emerging markets  I lived between Peru and Indonesia and  Kenya and I thought I was part of a wave  of things generally getting better so  the idea that global poverty is not  getting better  hits me to my core and it forces me to  ask my trillion dollar question in the  face of all of these development efforts  why aren't we making progress against  poverty and in that is the Western  influence I'm a part of helping or  hurting my favorite development  economist Jason Hickel has a shocking  idea what if aid all the bridges all the  bed nets is irrelevant or worse than  irrelevant what if it is nothing more  than a convincing PR play like Truman's  speech a distraction in this narrative  we get to be the good guys the West is  helping the poor catch up and everyone  gets to sleep at night knowing we are  doing what we can and we don't have to  do anything differently besides maybe  give a little bit more an aid per year  if our global economic system is working  to alleviate poverty but clearly it's  not working so what's going on here why  haven't we made progress against poverty  despite half a century of effort I want  to put that 130 billion in aid per year  in perspective that's a big number  but it's much smaller than the value  that flows in the opposite direction  from poor to rich in 2016 the global  financial integrity group published  shocking data on the ways that value is  extracted out of  four countries this is not trade flows  but ways that value is being transferred  sometimes illegally from poor to rich so  when Western governments give loans to  poor countries to the IMF we demand back  substantial interest rate exorbitant  interest rates and with the miracle of  compound interest many poor countries  are continuously crippled under their  debt burden some of these debts were  taken decades ago and the principle has  been long repaid but we keep demanding  back interest to put this debts in  perspective the amount that poor  countries spend on debt service each  year is a bigger number than the UN  projects is needed to eradicate global  poverty completely and when we loan on  top of interest we have structural  adjustment so that's when Western  governments lend to poor countries we  demand the poor countries adopt certain  economic policies and we tell them these  policies will help them develop but  really these are policies of free trade  and low tariffs that benefit our Western  corporations working there and most  nefariously of all we have trade miss  invoicing which is the very illegal yet  very prevalent practice of large  corporations misreporting trade prices  on our invoices in order to avoid local  government taxes there's a lot more ways  that value flows from poor to rich but  the point here is that our aid budget is  ridiculously small compared to all the  ways money is gushing out of poor  countries this is outrageous  especially because we've used our tiny  aid budget to fuel a cultural narrative  of our own benevolence and that  narrative hides the patterns of  extraction that are actively keeping  poor countries poor maybe this isn't all  that surprising given centuries of  Western lead extraction under  colonialism I would argue it's the same  extraction  hidden behind a PR bandaid of  development I work in development so  seeing these numbers is deeply troubling  and to be clear I don't think  development in and of itself is bad but  it shouldn't be used as a cover-up for  extractive global economic system  imposed by the West and I've realized I  need to get my head up out of the sand  and look at this straight on if I want  to do any good and if you want to join  me there's places to start large  companies give lip service to things  like ESG through impact and CSR but I  think we need a new code of ethics so  when you run large corporations in the  developing world do basic things like  pay your fair share of taxes and demand  the companies you work for do too and  for all of us in the broader West I want  to challenge us to rethink our role in  development and stop hiding behind a  narrative that the West is helping to be  really blunt if you're from a rich  country your privilege was built on the  backs of those in poor countries and  continues to be built on them we have to  get our story straight rich countries  aren't developing poor countries it's  the other way around thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

